ref 17592186544656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544896 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124684" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544896 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544896 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a0/a0ec6bc2ecc45a67d2a6340039eee6cbb090ed7d15776e75b94a6c6d910a86e5.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544896 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f9ba8fdb-9397-5097-95f0-204a59ce4162" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544896 #edb/ref 1074 "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $5$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/a0/a0ec6bc2ecc45a67d2a6340039eee6cbb090ed7d15776e75b94a6c6d910a86e5.png)\n\nTherefore, the total area bounded by the polar curve is \n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=5\\cdot A=\\frac{45}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
